A||N||6||bocefas||I'm going to paint my GT||||1||
Z||000000||bocefas||12-29-2001||09:36 PM||||Ok, after lots of deliberation, I decided to take on the task of painting my '86 GT myself.  I want to change the color from black with silver to monochrome forest green.  My question is:  How difficult is it to take the car apart, even take the ground effecs and mouldings off and paint everything seperately?  I think that would be the only way to ensure a good job.  A local body shop said they would do it for $3000...Uh, NO!!  I've painted before, I'm not scared of that at all..but I know some pieces are riveted on and I'm unsure how the mouldings are attached to the doors and fenders...I'd like to be able to reattach everything the right way, not with trim adhesive and silicone.  Any input would be appreciated.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||12-29-2001||09:50 PM||||The price quote you received is in the right ballpark.  Painting is the easy part, taking the panels and moldings off and putting them back on is the time consuming part, especially replacing all the riveted components back on.  Lots of screws, nuts and bolts to keep track of and rivets to replace.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bill Shenefelt||12-30-2001||07:52 AM||||I painted my 86 changing from the factory non-shiny gold to forrest green faint metallic.  Being afraid of the new epoxy paints, I stuck with laquer.  I removed only the airfoil on the trunk.  All else was just masked.  took 3/4 of a gal.  I primed with a sealer, then std laquer primer.  Came out great.  Laquer is not as durable as the new epoxys but still looks better to me.  I used a green similar to that seen on some of the older firebirds.  I forget the color.  I covered with a clear overcoat and trimmed rounded body sections and the edges of the fin with a very light spray of highlight gold coarse metalflake mixed in with the clear and then recoated with clear.  I hate an orangepeal finish whick as far as I can see is only totally avoidable with block sanded laquer.  <BR>So far 5 years with no problems.  Ps, always "trim" hard to reach areas and around edges of pannels with a round pattern several coats and then paint exterior to be sure it is not too thin and sands off in these areas.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bill Shenefelt||||reg||1
Z||000003||skitime||12-30-2001||08:21 AM||||I removed all the body panels except the fastback and windshield panels to paint my car.  It really is easy but plan on buying all new trim clips (Fiero Store). I used threaded rivets to reattach things where rivets went into steel.  That allows me to easily remove body parts witha screwdriver during future mods.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodrv6||12-30-2001||11:40 AM||||I took all the panels off my 84 to repaint it a few years ago.  The roof panel is the most difficult to remove due to the factory sealer around the windshield.  You have to be very careful not to break the panel where it goes downd either side of the windshield.  I didn't use any rivets to reassemble it.  I used screws and lock nuts in areas where I could get to both sides, and used aircraft nut plates for the blind spots.  I have a pnuematic screw gun that makes putting screws in a fast, easy job  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000005||JD86GT350||12-30-2001||11:45 AM||||oof, well I'll say the opposite of the other fella and tell you to stay away from laquer, you can get so much better paint nowadays.  I'd go visit your local paint jobber and hopefully they can provide you with all the info you need, including some direction what you'll want to spray.  Urethane = good||||reg||1
Z||000006||bocefas||12-30-2001||09:21 PM||||I've been told that base coat/clear coat is best for many reasons, the most attractive (to me anyway) is the ease of use and it's supposedly more flexible when cured than an enamel.  I am just a little leary of the dissambly of the car.  I've replaced the front fascia and the left fender no problem..and to tell you the truth I'll probably leave the rear clip and rear fascia on.  The door panels look simple enough to remove, and will ease the installation of new dew wipes I hope...it's the ground effects and the rocker panels that bother me.<BR>||||reg||1
